WEBVTT

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:21.031
Four days of work, three days off — on full pay. What sounded utopian a few years ago is now being tried in many countries. In Germany too, dozens of companies took part in a pilot project in which employees worked only four days a week for six months.

00:00:21.281 --> 00:00:47.567
Supporters report astonishing results. Staff are said to be more rested, less often ill and more focused at work. Many firms found that their productivity had hardly fallen, or had even risen. Meetings became shorter, unnecessary tasks fell away. At the same time, the firms hope the model will make them more attractive in the competition for skilled workers.

00:00:47.817 --> 00:01:12.430
Nor do the employees themselves experience the change as purely positive. Anyone expected to do the same work in four days instead of five often works in a more focused way on those days, but also under more pressure. Breaks get shorter, personal chats with colleagues rarer. Some report that in the first weeks they needed the free Friday mainly to recover.

00:01:12.680 --> 00:01:41.200
Critics counter that the model is far from suitable for every sector. In a hospital, a bakery or on a building site, the work cannot simply be done in less time. Anyone who works less there needs extra staff — and that is exactly what is already lacking. Some economists also warn that if a whole country works less, it could endanger prosperity in the long run.

00:01:41.450 --> 00:02:00.527
The truth probably lies, as so often, somewhere in the middle. The four-day week will not work everywhere, but it forces companies to ask themselves an uncomfortable question: how much of our working time is actually productive — and how much of it is just habit?
